This review supersedes the interpretation of the exported incident page, not its historical record. The counts below are the 4 October export snapshot. A later course engineering update supplies the incident disposition; that update is separate from the report data and from independent production verification.

## Current disposition: closed 05 Oct

The course engineering update on the Challenge page marks #4471 closed on 5 October:

- **Parser — FIXED:** `fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales`. The corresponding [parser issue](https://github.com/MMZ993/mega-s01e03/issues/1) is closed as completed.
- **Hint — WON’T FIX:** booking-v3 form retired. The corresponding [hint issue](https://github.com/MMZ993/mega-s01e03/issues/2) is closed as not planned, not falsely marked fixed.

These are the course desk's reported dispositions. We did not implement or independently verify the production change. A fresh, cache-bypassed download still matches the original export byte-for-byte; it contains historical reports, not new post-fix measurements.

## What the executable evidence shows

<ReviewEvidence />

The published counts come from the supplied `uniqueById`, `groupByCause`, `causeOf`, `tenantOf`, and `readAs` logic. The export has overlapping pages: count each report ID once, retaining its last occurrence, and filter to `mergedInto === 4471`. Identical repeats do not represent extra reports.

Cause follows the tenant's locale, booking-form version and report channel. The browser locale and intake label are not authoritative. Determine day-first ordering with `Intl.DateTimeFormat(...).formatToParts`, as the supplied code does; do not maintain a guessed locale list.

## Distinct problems in the historical reports

- **Parser:** the shared parser reads month-first although the tenant writes day-first. This is the majority cause belonging to the original incident. Its corrective contract is day-first parsing, with regression coverage for day-first app/email paths and booking-v2 forms; the later desk update reports the parser fixed. See [parser issue](https://github.com/MMZ993/mega-s01e03/issues/1).
- **Hint:** booking-v3 prints `MM/DD/YYYY`, but its form reads the tenant's day-first format. The hint and interpretation needed to agree. The desk retired booking-v3 rather than fixing this hint; a parser-only fix must not be mistaken for a hint fix. See [hint issue](https://github.com/MMZ993/mega-s01e03/issues/2).

The original cause table gave the hint group no ticket of its own. The exported incident's statement that there was one issue and one queued fix was therefore not supported by its own rendered evidence. Separate public issues now preserve both causes and their different reported closure reasons.

## Symptoms are not proven root causes

The desk read some reports as a one-day move, while the classifier files them under parser. The table preserves that count within parser, not in addition to it. This discrepancy merits investigation of expected/actual dates and timezone handling; it does not prove a third defect or justify changing the classifier's totals.

## Queue history is not arrival history

Reports begin in April; merging begins in July. The large merge spike represents consolidation of earlier reports, not an equally large surge of newly received reports. Keep received and merged series separate when describing impact. Reports continue through the export date; “known” is a workflow label, not evidence that impact stopped. The product's approximate user population is not a measured affected-user count.

## Change the desk's deduplication decision

Subject-only matching is not sufficient. The executable examples demonstrate both different subjects sharing a cause and identical subjects hiding distinct causes; missing tenant context cannot establish equivalence. See the proposed [replacement decision](./rendered/kb/decisions/0002-dedupe-by-cause/index.html).

Preserve accepted decision 0001 unchanged. Read the governing logic and data alongside narrative, check review age, and consult current queue evidence before acting on this snapshot. A new incident page does not itself change the desk's active workflow.

## Sources and privacy

Evidence: the course-provided KB export, specifically `causes.ts`, `locales.ts`, `CauseTable.svelte`, `QueueTimeline.svelte`, `dedupe-cases.ts`, `desk-notes.json`, and report/tenant exports. Public data here contains aggregate counts only; raw reports, tenant records and authentication data are intentionally excluded. Rendered charts and tables use the checked-in aggregate snapshot; they are not a live feed.

